// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. HOMOGENEOUS MIXTURES
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "matter_separation",
  "Homogeneous Mixtures",

  `
  <h2>Homogeneous Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Homogeneous Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>homogeneous mixture</strong> is a mixture that has a
    uniform composition throughout the sample at the scale being
    considered.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its components are distributed evenly enough that different portions
    of the mixture have the same composition, provided the mixture
    remains uniform.
  </p>

  <p>
    A homogeneous mixture contains more than one substance, but the
    components are not separated into visibly distinct portions
    throughout the mixture.
  </p>

  <h3>Solutions</h3>

  <p>
    A solution is a homogeneous mixture. It contains a solvent and
    one or more solutes.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Solvent:</strong> the substance that dissolves the solute.
    In a solution of salt in water, water is the solvent.</li>
    <li><strong>Solute:</strong> the substance dissolved in the solvent.
    In salt water, sodium chloride is the solute.</li>
    <li><strong>Solution:</strong> the homogeneous mixture formed when
    the solute is distributed throughout the solvent.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In a solution of salt in water, the salt dissolves and its ions
    become distributed throughout the water. A properly prepared solution
    has the same composition in equal portions taken from different
    places in the solution.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples of Homogeneous Mixtures</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Salt dissolved in water to form a uniform solution.</li>
    <li>Sugar dissolved in water to form a uniform solution.</li>
    <li>Air that is uniformly mixed at the scale being considered.</li>
    <li>Brass, an alloy consisting mainly of copper and zinc, when
    considered as a uniform alloy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These examples contain multiple substances. Their uniform appearance
    does not make them pure substances or compounds.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition Throughout the Sample</h3>

  <p>
    The key property is uniform composition. If equal portions are
    taken from different places in a uniform salt solution, each portion
    has the same salt-to-water ratio, within ordinary measurement limits.
  </p>

  <p>
    If additional water is poured into a salt solution and mixed
    thoroughly, the result can remain a homogeneous mixture, but its
    concentration changes.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Salt Dissolved in Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Salt is dissolved completely in water
  and stirred until the solution is uniform. Explain why it is a
  homogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substances.</p>
  <p>
    The solution contains water and dissolved sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the distribution of the components.</p>
  <p>
    When the salt has dissolved completely and the solution is uniform,
    the dissolved components are distributed throughout the liquid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The composition is uniform throughout the sample.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The salt solution is a homogeneous
  mixture because it contains water and dissolved salt distributed
  uniformly throughout the solution.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Sugar Solution</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A student dissolves sugar in water
  until no sugar crystals remain visible. Explain how the student can
  determine whether the resulting sample is homogeneous.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Inspect the sample.</p>
  <p>
    Check that no separate sugar crystals remain and that the solution
    appears uniform.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the composition at different positions.</p>
  <p>
    If the sugar is fully dissolved and the solution is mixed uniformly,
    equal portions taken from different positions have the same composition.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Classify the sample.</p>
  <p>
    A uniform sugar solution is a homogeneous mixture of sugar and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is homogeneous if the sugar
  is fully dissolved and the solution has uniform composition throughout.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Diluting a Salt Solution</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Water is added to a uniform salt solution
  and the mixture is stirred thoroughly. Explain what happens to its
  concentration and whether it can remain homogeneous.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what is added.</p>
  <p>
    Additional water increases the amount of solvent.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the amount of dissolved salt.</p>
  <p>
    If no salt is added or removed, the amount of dissolved salt remains
    the same while the total solution volume increases.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the effect on concentration.</p>
  <p>
    The salt becomes less concentrated because it is distributed through
    a larger amount of solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Determine whether the mixture remains uniform.</p>
  <p>
    If the salt remains dissolved and stirring produces a uniform
    solution, the diluted sample is still homogeneous.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution becomes less concentrated
  and can remain homogeneous if the dissolved salt is distributed
  uniformly throughout the water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 4: Air as a Homogeneous Mixture</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why a well-mixed sample of air
  can be classified as a homogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the components.</p>
  <p>
    Air contains nitrogen, oxygen, argon, carbon dioxide, and other gases.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider their distribution.</p>
  <p>
    In a well-mixed sample, the gases are distributed uniformly at
    the scale being considered.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The sample has a uniform composition even though it contains
    different gases.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Well-mixed air is a homogeneous mixture
  because several gases are uniformly distributed throughout the sample.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A homogeneous mixture has uniform composition throughout the sample.</li>
    <li>A solution is a homogeneous mixture.</li>
    <li>The solvent dissolves the solute.</li>
    <li>Homogeneous mixtures contain multiple substances and are not
    automatically pure substances or compounds.</li>
    <li>Dilution changes concentration but can leave the mixture homogeneous.</li>
    <li>Uniform appearance alone does not make a mixture a pure substance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Explain the uniformity of each sample. Distinguish uniform composition
    from the idea that a sample contains only one substance.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define a homogeneous mixture and explain what is meant by uniform composition throughout a sample.",
      hint: "Consider what would be found in equal portions taken from different places.",
      steps: [
        "Define a homogeneous mixture.",
        "Explain how its components are distributed.",
        "Describe what equal portions of the uniform sample contain."
      ],
      ans: "A homogeneous mixture is a mixture with uniform composition throughout the sample. Equal portions taken from different places have the same composition, within ordinary measurement limits.",
      why: "Uniform composition means that the components are evenly distributed at the scale being considered. The mixture still contains more than one substance."
    },
    {
      q: "In a salt solution, identify the solute and the solvent. Explain why the solution is classified as a homogeneous mixture when the salt is fully dissolved and uniformly distributed.",
      hint: "Identify which substance dissolves and which substance does the dissolving.",
      steps: [
        "Identify sodium chloride as the substance being dissolved.",
        "Identify water as the dissolving medium.",
        "Explain how the dissolved salt is distributed throughout the solution."
      ],
      ans: "Sodium chloride is the solute, and water is the solvent. The solution is homogeneous because the dissolved salt is distributed uniformly throughout the water.",
      why: "A solution is a homogeneous mixture. Its components remain present in a uniform physical combination rather than becoming one new compound."
    },
    {
      q: "A student takes equal volumes of a well-mixed sugar solution from the top and bottom of a container. Explain what should be true about the sugar concentration in the two portions and why.",
      hint: "Use the meaning of uniform composition.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the sample as a well-mixed solution.",
        "Apply the definition of a homogeneous mixture.",
        "Compare the composition of equal portions from different positions."
      ],
      ans: "The two portions should have the same sugar concentration, within ordinary measurement limits, because the sugar is uniformly distributed throughout the solution.",
      why: "A homogeneous solution has uniform composition throughout the sample when it is properly mixed and remains uniform."
    },
    {
      q: "Water is added to a uniform salt solution and the solution is stirred thoroughly. Explain how the concentration changes and why the resulting solution can still be homogeneous.",
      hint: "Track the amount of dissolved salt and the amount of water.",
      steps: [
        "State that additional water increases the amount of solvent.",
        "Assume no salt is added or removed and explain the effect on concentration.",
        "Explain what thorough mixing does to the distribution of the dissolved salt."
      ],
      ans: "The concentration decreases because the same amount of dissolved salt is distributed through a larger amount of solution. The solution can remain homogeneous if the salt stays dissolved and is uniformly distributed.",
      why: "Homogeneity refers to uniform composition, not to having a particular concentration. A diluted solution can still be uniform."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why well-mixed air can be a homogeneous mixture even though it contains several different gases.",
      hint: "Consider the distribution of the gases, not whether there is more than one gas.",
      steps: [
        "Name at least two gases present in air.",
        "Explain how they are distributed in a well-mixed sample.",
        "Connect the uniform distribution to the definition of a homogeneous mixture."
      ],
      ans: "Air contains several gases, such as nitrogen and oxygen, distributed uniformly in a well-mixed sample. It is therefore a homogeneous mixture at the scale being considered.",
      why: "A homogeneous mixture can contain several substances. What matters is that its composition is uniform throughout the sample."
    }
  ]
);
