add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_bonding",
  "Metallic Bonding",
  `<h2>Metallic Bonding</h2>

  <h3>1. What is metallic bonding?</h3>

  <p>Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between a lattice of positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.</p>

  <p>To understand this definition, identify the three important ideas:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive metal ions:</strong> metal particles that have lost some of their outer electrons.</li>
    <li><strong>Delocalised electrons:</strong> outer electrons that are not confined to one particular atom and can move throughout the metal structure.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrostatic attraction:</strong> the force of attraction between particles with opposite electrical charges.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Metallic bonding is not simply an attraction between neutral metal atoms. It is the attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons.</p>

  <h3>2. How metallic bonding forms</h3>

  <p>Consider a metal such as sodium. An isolated sodium atom has the electron arrangement 2,8,1. Its outer electron can become delocalised when sodium forms a metallic structure.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Metal atoms contribute some of their outer electrons to a shared pool of delocalised electrons.</li>
    <li>The atoms become positive metal ions because they have lost electrons.</li>
    <li>The positive ions arrange themselves in a regular, closely packed structure.</li>
    <li>The delocalised electrons move throughout the structure.</li>
    <li>The electrostatic attraction between the positive ions and negative electrons holds the structure together.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Important:</strong> the positive ions do not normally move freely through the solid metal. They occupy positions in the lattice, while the delocalised electrons can move through it.</p>

  <h3>3. Understanding the structure</h3>

  <pre>
Positive metal ions in a lattice

   M⁺       M⁺       M⁺
      e⁻  e⁻  e⁻
   M⁺       M⁺       M⁺
    e⁻  e⁻  e⁻  e⁻
   M⁺       M⁺       M⁺

M⁺ = positive metal ion
e⁻ = delocalised electron
  </pre>

  <p>This is a simplified model. The actual structure is three-dimensional, and the electrons are not confined to the spaces shown in the diagram.</p>

  <h3>4. Why the structure remains together</h3>

  <p>Each positive ion attracts the surrounding negative delocalised electrons. The electrons are spread throughout the structure, so their attraction helps hold the entire metallic lattice together.</p>

  <p>There is no requirement for each positive ion to be paired with one particular electron. The metallic bond is a collective attraction throughout the structure.</p>

  <h3>5. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Define metallic bonding.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the positively charged particles: positive metal ions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the negatively charged particles: delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the force between them: strong electrostatic attraction.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain how a sodium atom contributes to metallic bonding.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium has electron arrangement 2,8,1.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It contributes its one outer electron to the delocalised electron system.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The sodium particle becomes Na⁺ because it has lost one electron.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Na⁺ is attracted to the delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Each sodium atom contributes one outer electron to the delocalised electron system and forms a positive sodium ion. The attraction between the ions and electrons helps hold the metal together.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Correct the statement: “Metallic bonding is the attraction between negative metal ions and positive electrons.”</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Check the charge of the metal ions. They are positive, not negative.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Check the charge of electrons. Electrons are negative, not positive.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the attraction using the correct charges.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and negatively charged delocalised electrons.</p>

  <h3>6. Common misconception</h3>

  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Metallic bonding occurs because metal atoms share electrons in the same way as non-metal atoms in a covalent molecule.</p>

  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> In the metallic model, outer electrons become delocalised throughout the metal. The positive ions and the delocalised electrons are held together by electrostatic attraction.</p>`,

  [
    {
      q: "Define metallic bonding and identify the two types of particles involved.",
      hint: "Your definition should include the force, the positive particles and the electrons.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Metallic bonding is a type of electrostatic attraction.",
        "Step 2: The positive particles are metal ions.",
        "Step 3: The negative particles are delocalised electrons.",
        "Step 4: Combine these ideas into one precise definition."
      ],
      ans: "Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.",
      why: "The definition identifies the particles, their opposite charges and the force that holds the metallic structure together."
    },
    {
      q: "A sodium atom has the electron arrangement 2,8,1. Explain how it contributes to metallic bonding.",
      hint: "Focus on the outer electron and the charge left on the sodium particle.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Sodium has one electron in its outermost energy level.",
        "Step 2: This electron can become delocalised in the metallic structure.",
        "Step 3: Losing the electron leaves the sodium particle with a net charge of +1.",
        "Step 4: The resulting Na⁺ ion is attracted to the delocalised electrons."
      ],
      ans: "Sodium contributes its one outer electron to the delocalised electron system and forms Na⁺. Electrostatic attraction between Na⁺ ions and the delocalised electrons holds the structure together.",
      why: "The outer electron becomes part of the shared electron system, leaving a positive ion in the metallic lattice."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why the metallic structure contains positive ions rather than only neutral metal atoms.",
      hint: "What happens to the charge of an atom when it loses a negatively charged electron?",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: An electron carries a negative charge.",
        "Step 2: When a metal atom loses an electron, it loses negative charge.",
        "Step 3: The remaining particle has an overall positive charge.",
        "Step 4: This particle is a positive metal ion."
      ],
      ans: "The metal atoms lose some of their outer electrons, leaving positively charged metal ions.",
      why: "Removing negatively charged electrons leaves the remaining particle with more positive charge than negative charge."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner says that each positive metal ion is bonded to just one delocalised electron. Explain why this model is incorrect.",
      hint: "Think about whether the delocalised electrons belong to individual ions.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Delocalised electrons are not restricted to one particular metal ion.",
        "Step 2: They can move throughout the metallic structure.",
        "Step 3: Each ion is attracted to the surrounding delocalised electron system.",
        "Step 4: Metallic bonding is therefore a collective attraction throughout the structure."
      ],
      ans: "The electrons are delocalised throughout the structure rather than being paired individually with specific ions. The positive ions are attracted to the shared electron system.",
      why: "The collective nature of the attraction distinguishes metallic bonding from a model involving individual ion–electron pairs."
    },
    {
      q: "A solid metal contains positive ions and delocalised electrons. Explain what holds its structure together.",
      hint: "Use the charges of the particles to identify the force.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: The metal ions have positive charges.",
        "Step 2: The delocalised electrons have negative charges.",
        "Step 3: Opposite electrical charges attract.",
        "Step 4: This strong electrostatic attraction holds the metallic structure together."
      ],
      ans: "Strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons holds the structure together.",
      why: "The metallic lattice is stabilised by attraction between its positively charged ions and its negatively charged delocalised electrons."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_bonding",
  "Delocalised Electrons in Metals",
  `<h2>Delocalised Electrons in Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of delocalised electrons</h3>

  <p>Delocalised electrons are electrons that are not confined to one particular atom or bond. In a metal, they can move throughout the metallic structure.</p>

  <p>In the metallic bonding model, these electrons usually originate from the outer energy levels of metal atoms.</p>

  <h3>2. How delocalisation occurs</h3>

  <p>When many metal atoms form a metallic structure, their outer electrons can become part of a shared electron system.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Metal atoms contribute outer electrons.</li>
    <li>The atoms become positive metal ions.</li>
    <li>The ions form an ordered lattice in the solid metal.</li>
    <li>The delocalised electrons extend throughout the structure and can move through it.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Delocalisation does not mean that electrons have no attraction to the metal. The electrons remain attracted to the positive ions throughout the structure.</p>

  <h3>3. Ions and electrons behave differently</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive metal ions:</strong> occupy positions in the solid lattice and vibrate about those positions.</li>
    <li><strong>Delocalised electrons:</strong> are mobile and can move through the metal.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>This difference explains why electrons, rather than the fixed metal ions, carry electrical charge through an ordinary solid metal.</p>

  <h3>4. Electrical conductivity</h3>

  <p>Electrical current requires charged particles that can move. When a potential difference is applied across a metal, its delocalised electrons can drift through the structure and carry charge.</p>

  <p>The electrons already exist in the metal; the power supply does not need to create them. It establishes an electric field that drives their net movement.</p>

  <h3>5. Thermal conductivity</h3>

  <p>Metals also transfer thermal energy efficiently. Mobile electrons can carry energy through the structure and transfer it through interactions with other electrons and the lattice.</p>

  <p>Vibrations of the lattice also transfer thermal energy. Therefore, it is more accurate to say that mobile electrons make an important contribution to heat conduction than to say they are the only mechanism.</p>

  <h3>6. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explain why delocalised electrons are different from electrons confined to an individual atom.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An electron confined to an individual atom belongs to that atom's electron arrangement.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A delocalised electron is not restricted to one particular atom.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In a metal, delocalised electrons can move throughout the structure.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Delocalised electrons are not confined to individual metal atoms and can move throughout the metallic structure.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain how delocalised electrons enable a metal to conduct electricity.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Electrical current is the flow of electric charge.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Delocalised electrons carry negative charge.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> When a potential difference is applied, the electrons acquire a net drift through the metal.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Their movement carries charge from one region to another.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Delocalised electrons are mobile charge carriers. When a potential difference is applied, their net movement through the metal produces an electric current.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explain why the positive ions in a solid metal do not carry the current in the same way as the electrons.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The metal ions are positively charged.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In the solid lattice, they occupy fixed average positions and mainly vibrate about them.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> They do not flow freely through the solid metal.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The delocalised electrons are mobile and carry the current.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The positive ions are held at lattice positions, whereas the delocalised electrons can move through the metal and carry electric charge.</p>

  <h3>7. Common misconception</h3>

  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The positive ions flow through a solid metal when it conducts electricity.</p>

  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> In an ordinary solid metal, the positive ions remain around their lattice positions. The delocalised electrons provide the mobile charge carriers.</p>`,

  [
    {
      q: "Define a delocalised electron in the context of a metal.",
      hint: "Explain whether it is restricted to one atom.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Consider where an ordinary atom's electrons are associated.",
        "Step 2: A delocalised electron is not confined to one particular metal atom.",
        "Step 3: It can move throughout the metallic structure."
      ],
      ans: "A delocalised electron is an electron that is not confined to one particular atom and can move throughout the metallic structure.",
      why: "The ability to move throughout the structure is central to the role of delocalised electrons in metals."
    },
    {
      q: "A metal wire is connected to a cell and a lamp lights. Explain the role of delocalised electrons.",
      hint: "Relate mobile electrons to the meaning of electric current.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Electric current is the movement of electric charge.",
        "Step 2: Delocalised electrons carry negative charge.",
        "Step 3: The cell establishes a potential difference across the wire.",
        "Step 4: The electric field causes a net drift of the mobile electrons.",
        "Step 5: This movement carries current through the wire."
      ],
      ans: "The potential difference causes delocalised electrons to drift through the metal, carrying electric charge and producing current.",
      why: "The mobile electrons act as charge carriers in the metallic conductor."
    },
    {
      q: "Describe the movement of positive metal ions in a solid metal when it conducts electricity.",
      hint: "Distinguish vibrating about fixed positions from flowing through the structure.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Positive metal ions occupy positions in the solid lattice.",
        "Step 2: They vibrate about their average positions.",
        "Step 3: They do not normally flow freely through the solid.",
        "Step 4: Delocalised electrons provide the mobile charge carriers."
      ],
      ans: "The positive ions vibrate about their lattice positions but do not flow freely through the solid. The delocalised electrons carry the electric current.",
      why: "Electrical conduction in a solid metal depends on mobile electrons, not on the bulk movement of its positive ions."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain one way delocalised electrons contribute to the thermal conductivity of a metal.",
      hint: "Think about how energy can be transferred from a hotter region to a cooler region.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Thermal energy is transferred from hotter regions to cooler regions.",
        "Step 2: Delocalised electrons are mobile throughout the metal.",
        "Step 3: They can carry energy and transfer it through interactions with other electrons and the lattice.",
        "Step 4: This contributes to efficient heat transfer."
      ],
      ans: "Mobile delocalised electrons carry thermal energy through the metal and transfer it through interactions with other electrons and the lattice.",
      why: "Mobile electrons contribute significantly to the thermal conductivity of metals, although lattice vibrations also transfer energy."
    },
    {
      q: "A student claims that delocalised electrons are completely detached from the metal and no longer attracted to its ions. Explain the error.",
      hint: "Distinguish freedom to move from absence of electrostatic attraction.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Delocalised means the electrons are not confined to individual atoms.",
        "Step 2: It does not mean that the electrons have left the metal.",
        "Step 3: The electrons remain within the metallic structure.",
        "Step 4: They remain electrostatically attracted to the positive ions."
      ],
      ans: "Delocalised electrons can move throughout the metal, but they remain within the structure and are attracted to its positive metal ions.",
      why: "Electron mobility and electrostatic attraction coexist in metallic bonding."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_bonding",
  "Metallic Bonding and Metal Properties",
  `<h2>Metallic Bonding and Metal Properties</h2>

  <h3>1. How structure determines properties</h3>

  <p>The properties of a metal are related to its structure: positive metal ions occupy lattice positions, while delocalised electrons move throughout the structure.</p>

  <p>Different properties arise from different features of this structure. Electrical conductivity depends on mobile electrons; malleability depends on how the lattice can change shape; and melting requires the metallic structure to be disrupted.</p>

  <h3>2. Electrical conductivity</h3>

  <p>Metals conduct electricity because they contain mobile delocalised electrons.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>A metal contains delocalised electrons that carry negative charge.</li>
    <li>A potential difference establishes an electric field through the metal.</li>
    <li>The electrons acquire a net drift through the structure.</li>
    <li>The movement of charge produces an electric current.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>3. Thermal conductivity</h3>

  <p>Mobile electrons transfer thermal energy through the metal. Lattice vibrations also contribute to heat transfer.</p>

  <p>In many metals, mobile electrons are an important reason that heat spreads quickly through the material.</p>

  <h3>4. Malleability</h3>

  <p><strong>Malleability</strong> is the ability of a material to be hammered or pressed into sheets without breaking.</p>

  <p>When a force is applied to a metal, layers of ions can shift relative to one another. The delocalised electrons continue to attract the positive ions, allowing metallic bonding to persist as the structure changes shape.</p>

  <p>This does not mean that metals can be deformed without limit. A sufficiently large force can damage or fracture a metal.</p>

  <h3>5. Ductility</h3>

  <p><strong>Ductility</strong> is the ability of a material to be drawn into wires.</p>

  <p>Many metals are ductile because their metallic structure can undergo substantial deformation while the metallic bonding remains effective. The layers and lattice positions can rearrange instead of the material immediately separating into individual particles.</p>

  <h3>6. Melting and boiling points</h3>

  <p>Metallic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons. Energy must be supplied to disrupt the metallic structure during melting.</p>

  <p>Many metals have high melting points, but the values vary widely. Metallic bond strength and structure differ between metals, so it is not correct to claim that every metal has a high melting point.</p>

  <p>Boiling a metal requires much more extensive separation of its particles than melting it. Many metals therefore also have high boiling points.</p>

  <h3>7. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explain why copper conducts electricity.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Copper has a metallic structure containing delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> These electrons carry negative charge and are mobile.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A potential difference causes a net drift of electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper conducts electricity because its delocalised electrons can move through the metal and carry electric charge.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain why a metal can be hammered into a sheet.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Hammering applies a force to the metallic lattice.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Layers of metal ions can shift relative to one another.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The delocalised electrons continue to attract the positive ions as the structure changes shape.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The metal can flatten instead of immediately breaking apart.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Metals are malleable because their layers can shift while metallic bonding continues to hold the structure together.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explain why many metals have high melting points.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Positive metal ions and delocalised electrons are held together by electrostatic attraction.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Melting requires energy to disrupt the ordered metallic structure.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Where the metallic attraction is strong, substantial energy is required.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Many metals have high melting points because considerable energy is needed to disrupt their metallic structures. The exact melting point depends on the metal.</p>

  <h3>8. Common misconception</h3>

  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Metals are malleable because metallic bonds are weak.</p>

  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> Metallic bonding can be strong. Malleability arises because the metallic structure can change shape as layers shift, while the attraction between the positive ions and delocalised electrons continues.</p>`,

  [
    {
      q: "Explain why metals conduct electricity, using the terms delocalised electrons and electric charge.",
      hint: "Describe what happens when a potential difference is applied.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Metals contain delocalised electrons.",
        "Step 2: These electrons carry negative electric charge.",
        "Step 3: A potential difference produces an electric field in the metal.",
        "Step 4: The electrons acquire a net drift through the structure.",
        "Step 5: The movement of charge forms an electric current."
      ],
      ans: "Metals conduct electricity because mobile delocalised electrons carry electric charge through the structure when a potential difference is applied.",
      why: "Electrical conductivity depends on the availability of mobile charge carriers."
    },
    {
      q: "Define malleability and explain why metallic bonding allows many metals to be hammered into sheets.",
      hint: "Include both the meaning of malleability and the movement of the layers.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Malleability is the ability to be hammered or pressed into sheets without breaking.",
        "Step 2: A force can cause layers of metal ions to shift relative to one another.",
        "Step 3: Delocalised electrons continue to attract the positive ions.",
        "Step 4: The metallic structure can change shape while the bonding remains effective."
      ],
      ans: "Malleability is the ability to be hammered into sheets. Metals are malleable because layers of ions can shift while the delocalised electrons continue to attract the positive ions.",
      why: "The metallic structure can deform without requiring the layers to separate completely."
    },
    {
      q: "Distinguish between malleability and ductility.",
      hint: "Think about the final shape produced in each case.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Malleability describes deformation into sheets by hammering or pressing.",
        "Step 2: Ductility describes the ability to be drawn into wires.",
        "Step 3: Both properties involve the ability of a metal to deform without immediately breaking."
      ],
      ans: "Malleability is the ability to be hammered or pressed into sheets, whereas ductility is the ability to be drawn into wires.",
      why: "The terms describe different forms of permanent deformation."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why many metals have high melting points, and state why the claim does not apply equally to every metal.",
      hint: "Connect melting to the metallic structure and compare the strength of bonding in different metals.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: Metallic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons.",
        "Step 2: Energy must be supplied to disrupt the metallic structure during melting.",
        "Step 3: Strong metallic attraction generally requires more energy to overcome.",
        "Step 4: Different metals have different structures and metallic bond strengths.",
        "Step 5: Therefore, melting points vary between metals."
      ],
      ans: "Many metals have high melting points because energy is needed to disrupt their metallic structures. However, melting points vary because metallic bonding and structure differ between metals.",
      why: "The strength of the metallic attraction helps explain melting behaviour, but it does not make all metals have the same or uniformly high melting point."
    },
    {
      q: "A student says, “Copper conducts electricity because its positive ions flow from one end of the wire to the other.” Correct the explanation.",
      hint: "Identify which particles are mobile in a solid metallic lattice.",
      steps: [
        "Step 1: The positive copper ions occupy lattice positions in the solid.",
        "Step 2: They vibrate about their average positions rather than flowing freely through the wire.",
        "Step 3: Copper contains mobile delocalised electrons.",
        "Step 4: These electrons drift through the metal when a potential difference is applied.",
        "Step 5: Their movement carries electric charge."
      ],
      ans: "Copper conducts electricity because mobile delocalised electrons move through the solid metal and carry charge, while the positive ions remain around their lattice positions.",
      why: "The mobile electrons, not the bulk movement of positive ions, provide electrical conduction in a solid copper wire."
    }
  ]
);

