add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_formulae",
  "Formulae of Covalent Compounds",

  `
  <h2>Formulae of Covalent Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Covalent Formula</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>covalent compound</strong> contains atoms joined by
    covalent bonds, which form when atoms share pairs of electrons.
    Its chemical formula uses element symbols and subscripts to show
    the types and numbers of atoms in a molecule.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike the formula of an ionic compound, which represents the
    simplest ratio of ions, a molecular formula for a covalent
    substance shows the actual number of each type of atom in
    one molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Reading Subscripts in Covalent Formulae</h3>

  <p>
    A subscript is the small number written after an element symbol.
    It tells you how many atoms of that element are represented
    in the molecule.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      H<sub>2</sub>O contains 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom.
    </li>
    <li>
      CO<sub>2</sub> contains 1 carbon atom and 2 oxygen atoms.
    </li>
    <li>
      NH<sub>3</sub> contains 1 nitrogen atom and 3 hydrogen atoms.
    </li>
    <li>
      If an element has no subscript, its number of atoms is 1.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Read each subscript as applying only to the element symbol
    immediately before it. Do not treat a subscript as the total
    number of atoms in the entire molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Writing a Covalent Formula from the Given Atom Numbers</h3>

  <p>
    When the numbers of atoms in a molecule are given, write the
    correct element symbols and place each atom count as a subscript.
    Omit a subscript of 1.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not automatically use the valency-crossing method to produce
    every covalent molecular formula. The formula must represent the
    actual molecule or the atom numbers specified in the question.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Interpret H2O</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many atoms of each element are present in H<sub>2</sub>O?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the element symbols.</p>
  <p>H represents hydrogen; O represents oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the subscripts.</p>
  <p>
    H has subscript 2, so there are two hydrogen atoms.
    O has no written subscript, so there is one oxygen atom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, giving 3 atoms in total.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Interpret CO2</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many atoms of each element are present in CO<sub>2</sub>?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the symbols.</p>
  <p>C represents carbon; O represents oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count each type of atom.</p>
  <p>
    C has no subscript, so there is one carbon atom.
    O has subscript 2, so there are two oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Find the total number of atoms.</p>
  <p>1 + 2 = 3 atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> One carbon atom and two oxygen atoms, 3 atoms in total.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Write a formula from atom counts</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A molecule contains two nitrogen atoms and three hydrogen atoms. Write its formula.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the symbols.</p>
  <p>N represents nitrogen; H represents hydrogen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add the atom counts as subscripts.</p>
  <p>
    Two nitrogen atoms give N<sub>2</sub>.
    Three hydrogen atoms give H<sub>3</sub>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Combine the symbols in the stated order.</p>
  <p>N<sub>2</sub>H<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> N<sub>2</sub>H<sub>3</sub> represents the atom counts specified in the question. This exercise constructs a formula from given counts; it does not establish that the species is a stable, ordinary compound.</p>

  <h3>4. Avoiding Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not omit a subscript when more than one atom of that element
      is present.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not write a subscript 1. One atom is understood when no
      subscript appears.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse the number of atoms of one element with the
      total number of atoms in a molecule.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not change the atom counts in a given molecular formula
      by simplifying its subscripts.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use the actual molecular formula supplied or established for
      the substance, rather than assuming all covalent formulae can
      be generated by crossing valencies.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    A covalent molecular formula identifies the elements in a molecule
    and shows how many atoms of each element it contains. Read every
    subscript carefully, and remember that a missing subscript means 1.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Explain what the formula H2O tells you about the numbers of hydrogen and oxygen atoms in one molecule.",
      hint: "Read the subscript after H and check whether O has a subscript.",
      steps: [
        "H represents hydrogen and has subscript 2.",
        "Therefore, the molecule contains two hydrogen atoms.",
        "O represents oxygen and has no written subscript.",
        "Therefore, the molecule contains one oxygen atom."
      ],
      ans: "One H2O molecule contains two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.",
      why: "Subscripts show the number of atoms of the element immediately before them; no subscript means one."
    },
    {
      q: "A molecule has the formula CO2. State the number of carbon atoms, the number of oxygen atoms and the total number of atoms.",
      hint: "Count each element separately before adding the numbers.",
      steps: [
        "C has no subscript, so there is one carbon atom.",
        "O has subscript 2, so there are two oxygen atoms.",
        "Add the counts: 1 + 2 = 3."
      ],
      ans: "1 carbon atom, 2 oxygen atoms and 3 atoms in total.",
      why: "The total is found by adding the counts of all atoms represented in the formula."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the formula representing a molecule containing one nitrogen atom and three hydrogen atoms.",
      hint: "Use N for nitrogen and H for hydrogen. Remember that a subscript of 1 is omitted.",
      steps: [
        "Write N for one nitrogen atom.",
        "Write H for hydrogen.",
        "Place subscript 3 after H to show three hydrogen atoms.",
        "Do not add a subscript after N because its count is one."
      ],
      ans: "NH3.",
      why: "The formula represents one nitrogen atom and three hydrogen atoms."
    },
    {
      q: "A student says that CO2 contains two carbon atoms and one oxygen atom. Identify the mistake and give the correct interpretation.",
      hint: "Check which symbol is immediately before the subscript 2.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the symbol before the subscript 2.",
        "The subscript follows O, not C.",
        "C has no subscript, so its count is one.",
        "O has subscript 2, so its count is two."
      ],
      ans: "The student has assigned the subscript to the wrong element. CO2 contains one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.",
      why: "A subscript applies to the element symbol immediately before it."
    },
    {
      q: "A molecule is specified to contain two carbon atoms, four hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Write the formula using the symbols C, H and O, and calculate the total number of atoms.",
      hint: "Write each element symbol with its stated count as a subscript, omitting a subscript of 1.",
      steps: [
        "Two carbon atoms are represented by C2.",
        "Four hydrogen atoms are represented by H4.",
        "One oxygen atom is represented by O, with no subscript.",
        "Combine the symbols in the stated order: C2H4O.",
        "Add the counts: 2 + 4 + 1 = 7."
      ],
      ans: "C2H4O, with 7 atoms in total.",
      why: "The formula follows the specified atom counts. This construction exercise does not, by itself, establish the identity or stability of a particular compound."
    }
  ]
);
