add(
  "chemistry",
  "acids_bases_salts",
  "Reactions of Acids with Metal Oxides",
  `
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Metal Oxides</h2>

  <h3>1. The reaction pattern</h3>

  <p>
    Many basic metal oxides react with acids to form a <strong>salt
    and water</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Metal Oxide + Acid → Salt + Water</p>

  <p>
    The salt formed depends on the metal oxide and the acid used.
    For example, copper(II) oxide reacting with sulfuric acid forms
    copper(II) sulfate and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    This screen focuses on basic metal oxides. Not all metal oxides
    have identical chemical behaviour, so do not apply this reaction
    pattern indiscriminately to every oxide.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Copper(II) oxide and sulfuric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for copper(II) oxide reacting with
    sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Copper(II) oxide contains copper, and sulfuric acid supplies the
    sulfate ion. The salt is copper(II) sulfate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Predict the other product.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Water is formed when the basic oxide reacts with the acid.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    CuO(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: Cu = 1, S = 1, H = 2 and O = 5 on both sides.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Magnesium oxide and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products and balance the equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Magnesium chloride is the salt.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water is the other product.</p>

  <p>
    Start with:
    MgO + HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p>
    There are two chlorine atoms in MgCl<sub>2</sub>, so place 2 before HCl.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    MgO(s) + 2HCl(aq) →
    MgCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Identifying products</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner reacts zinc oxide with dilute sulfuric acid. Identify the
    salt and write the balanced equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Zinc oxide provides zinc.</p>
  <p>
    Sulfuric acid supplies the sulfate ion, so the salt is zinc sulfate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add water as the other product.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    ZnO(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    ZnSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: one zinc atom, one sulfur atom, two hydrogen atoms and five
    oxygen atoms appear on each side.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Understanding the observations</h3>

  <p>
    A metal oxide may gradually disappear as it reacts with the acid.
    The salt may remain dissolved if it is soluble. If excess solid
    remains after the reaction, it may need to be separated from the
    solution when preparing a salt.
  </p>

  <p>
    The key distinction for this screen is the product pattern:
    a basic metal oxide reacts with an acid to form a salt and water,
    not hydrogen gas or carbon dioxide.
  </p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "State the general word equation for the reaction between a basic metal oxide and an acid.",
      hint: "This reaction has two main products.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the two reactants.",
        "Recall the salt formed from the metal oxide and acid.",
        "Name the second product."
      ],
      ans: "Metal Oxide + Acid → Salt + Water",
      why: "A basic metal oxide neutralises an acid to form a salt and water."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the balanced equation for copper(II) oxide reacting with sulfuric acid.",
      hint: "The salt is copper(II) sulfate.",
      steps: [
        "Write copper(II) sulfate as the salt.",
        "Add water as the second product.",
        "Check the number of atoms on both sides."
      ],
      ans: "CuO + H2SO4 → CuSO4 + H2O",
      why: "Copper, sulfur, hydrogen and oxygen atoms balance without changing any coefficients."
    },
    {
      q: "Write and balance the equation for magnesium oxide reacting with hydrochloric acid.",
      hint: "The salt is magnesium chloride, which contains two chlorine atoms.",
      steps: [
        "Write MgO + HCl → MgCl2 + H2O.",
        "Place 2 before HCl to balance chlorine.",
        "Check hydrogen, oxygen, magnesium and chlorine."
      ],
      ans: "MgO + 2HCl → MgCl2 + H2O",
      why: "The coefficient 2 supplies the two chlorine atoms needed for magnesium chloride and also balances hydrogen."
    },
    {
      q: "Zinc oxide reacts with sulfuric acid. Name the salt and write the balanced equation.",
      hint: "Combine the zinc from the oxide with the sulfate from the acid.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the salt as zinc sulfate.",
        "Add water as the other product.",
        "Check all atoms."
      ],
      ans: "The salt is zinc sulfate. ZnO + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2O.",
      why: "Zinc oxide reacting with sulfuric acid forms zinc sulfate and water, with all atoms balanced."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain how the products of an acid-metal oxide reaction differ from those of an acid-carbonate reaction.",
      hint: "Compare the complete product patterns for the two reactions.",
      steps: [
        "State the products of an acid reacting with a basic metal oxide.",
        "State the products of an acid reacting with a metal carbonate.",
        "Identify the product that distinguishes the carbonate reaction."
      ],
      ans: "A basic metal oxide reacts with an acid to form a salt and water. A metal carbonate reacts with an acid to form a salt, water and carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide is the additional product in the carbonate reaction.",
      why: "Recognising the product patterns helps distinguish the two types of acid reactions."
    }
  ]
);
