add(
  "chemistry",
  "acids_bases_salts",
  "Strong and Weak Acids",
  `
  <h2>Strong and Weak Acids</h2>

  <h3>1. What determines acid strength?</h3>

  <p>
    The strength of an acid depends on how completely it ionises in
    water, not simply on how concentrated its solution is.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <strong>strong acid</strong> ionises essentially completely in
    water. A <strong>weak acid</strong> ionises only partially, so an
    equilibrium is established between the un-ionised acid and its ions.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Strong acids</h3>

  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid is an example of a strong acid. In water, it
    ionises essentially completely:
  </p>

  <p>
    HCl(aq) → H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    This is a simplified school-level representation. In water,
    hydrogen ions are associated with water molecules as hydronium ions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Weak acids</h3>

  <p>
    Ethanoic acid is an example of a weak acid. It ionises only
    partially in water:
  </p>

  <p>
    CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) ⇌
    H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    The double arrow indicates that the reaction is reversible and
    that an equilibrium is established. Most ethanoic acid molecules
    remain un-ionised in a typical dilute aqueous solution.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Strength is not concentration</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Strength</strong> describes the extent of ionisation.
    <strong>Concentration</strong> describes the amount of dissolved
    substance per unit volume of solution.
  </p>

  <p>
    A concentrated weak acid can contain more acid per unit volume
    than a very dilute strong acid. Therefore, the words strong and
    concentrated do not mean the same thing.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid ionises essentially completely in water.
    Classify it as strong or weak.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the definition of a strong acid.</p>
  <p>
    A strong acid ionises essentially completely in water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the given information with the definition.</p>
  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid ionises essentially completely.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hydrochloric acid is a strong acid.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting ethanoic acid ionisation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ethanoic acid is represented by the equation:
  </p>

  <p>
    CH<sub>3</sub>COOH ⇌
    H<sup>+</sup> + CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>−</sup>
  </p>

  <p>
    Explain why the double arrow is appropriate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall that a weak acid ionises partially.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some acid molecules ionise, while some ions recombine.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> The double arrow represents reversible,
    partial ionisation and the establishment of equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Strength versus concentration</strong></p>

  <p>
    Solution A is a concentrated weak acid. Solution B is a very dilute
    strong acid. Can you determine which has the greater acid
    concentration from strength alone?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what strength measures.</p>
  <p>
    Strength describes the extent of ionisation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify what concentration measures.</p>
  <p>
    Concentration describes the amount of dissolved acid per unit volume.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The descriptions of strength alone
  are insufficient to determine which solution has the greater
  concentration.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Strong acid: essentially complete ionisation.</li>
    <li>Weak acid: partial ionisation.</li>
    <li>Strength and concentration describe different properties.</li>
  </ul>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "Define a strong acid in terms of its ionisation in water.",
      hint: "Think about whether most acid molecules ionise or remain un-ionised.",
      steps: [
        "Recall what acid strength describes.",
        "State the extent of ionisation of a strong acid."
      ],
      ans: "A strong acid ionises essentially completely in water.",
      why: "Strong acids produce their ions to a much greater extent than weak acids under comparable conditions."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why ethanoic acid is classified as a weak acid.",
      hint: "Consider the extent of ionisation and the meaning of the double arrow.",
      steps: [
        "Write the reversible ionisation of ethanoic acid.",
        "Explain what the double arrow represents.",
        "Connect partial ionisation to the definition of a weak acid."
      ],
      ans: "Ethanoic acid is weak because it ionises only partially in water and establishes an equilibrium between molecules and ions.",
      why: "A weak acid does not ionise completely; a significant proportion of its molecules remain un-ionised."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the simplified ionisation equation for hydrochloric acid in water.",
      hint: "Identify the hydrogen ion and chloride ion.",
      steps: [
        "Write the formula of hydrochloric acid.",
        "Identify the ions formed.",
        "Use a single arrow to represent essentially complete ionisation."
      ],
      ans: "HCl(aq) → H+(aq) + Cl−(aq)",
      why: "The equation represents the essentially complete ionisation of hydrochloric acid in water using the school-level hydrogen-ion notation."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the ionisation equation for ethanoic acid and explain why it uses a double arrow.",
      hint: "Ethanoic acid has the formula CH3COOH.",
      steps: [
        "Write CH3COOH on the left.",
        "Write H+ and CH3COO− on the right.",
        "Use a double arrow to show reversible partial ionisation."
      ],
      ans: "CH3COOH(aq) ⇌ H+(aq) + CH3COO−(aq). The double arrow shows reversible partial ionisation and equilibrium.",
      why: "Weak acids establish an equilibrium because only a proportion of the acid molecules ionise."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain the difference between a strong acid and a concentrated acid.",
      hint: "One term concerns ionisation; the other concerns amount per unit volume.",
      steps: [
        "Define acid strength.",
        "Define concentration.",
        "Explain why the terms cannot be used interchangeably."
      ],
      ans: "A strong acid ionises essentially completely in water. A concentrated acid contains a relatively large amount of dissolved acid per unit volume. These describe different properties.",
      why: "A solution may be strong but dilute, or weak but concentrated."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SCREEN 13: STRONG AND WEAK BASES
