// SCREEN 9: CATALYSTS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "energy_rate_reaction",
  "Catalysts",

  `
  <h2>Catalysts</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Catalyst</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>catalyst</strong> is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical
    reaction without being permanently consumed by the overall reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst may take part in intermediate steps of the reaction, but it is
    regenerated by the end of the catalytic process. It can therefore continue
    helping the reaction proceed, provided it remains active and suitable conditions
    are maintained.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst does not have to be present in the same physical state as every
    reactant. Some catalysts are solids that interact with gaseous or dissolved
    reactants at their surfaces, while others operate in solution.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How a Catalyst Increases Reaction Rate</h3>

  <p>
    A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation
    energy than the uncatalysed pathway.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the same temperature, a greater proportion of reacting particles can overcome
    the lower activation-energy barrier. This increases the number of successful
    reactions per second and usually increases the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst does not simply supply energy to every reacting particle. It changes
    the pathway by which the reaction can occur, reducing the energy barrier that
    must be overcome.
  </p>

  <h3>3. What a Catalyst Does Not Change</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the chemical equation for the overall reaction.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the overall energy difference between the reactants and products for the same reaction.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the equilibrium position of a reversible reaction; it speeds up the forward and reverse reactions so equilibrium is reached sooner.</li>
    <li>A catalyst is not normally used up permanently by the overall reaction, although it can become inactive or be lost through side processes.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For an irreversible reaction under fixed conditions, a catalyst generally helps
    the reaction reach its attainable final amount more quickly. It does not create
    additional reactant or automatically increase the theoretical yield.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Examples of Catalysts</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Manganese(IV) oxide:</strong> Manganese(IV) oxide, MnO₂, catalyses the
    decomposition of hydrogen peroxide. Hydrogen peroxide decomposes into water
    and oxygen:
  </p>

  <p>2H₂O₂(aq) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Iron:</strong> Finely divided iron is used as a catalyst in the Haber
    process for making ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Biological enzymes:</strong> Enzymes act as biological catalysts. They
    speed up particular reactions in living organisms. Their activity depends on
    conditions such as temperature and pH.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Comparing Catalysed and Uncatalysed Reactions</h3>

  <p>
    When comparing two pathways for the same reaction, the catalysed pathway has
    a lower activation energy. If temperature and other conditions are comparable,
    the lower barrier allows more particles to react successfully per second.
  </p>

  <p>
    A fair experiment comparing a reaction with and without a catalyst should use
    the same quantities and concentrations of reactants, the same temperature and
    the same measurement method. The presence or absence of the catalyst should
    be the main changed variable.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining the action of a catalyst</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction proceeds faster when a suitable catalyst is added. Explain the
    effect of the catalyst in terms of activation energy.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the change. The catalyst provides an alternative
  reaction pathway.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare activation energies. The catalysed pathway has
  a lower activation energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect to successful collisions. At the same temperature,
  a greater proportion of particles can overcome the lower barrier.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The catalyst increases the reaction rate by providing
  a pathway with lower activation energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating activation-energy reduction</strong></p>

  <p>
    An uncatalysed pathway has an activation energy of 150 kJ, while the catalysed
    pathway has an activation energy of 95 kJ. Calculate the reduction in activation
    energy.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the original activation energy: 150 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the catalysed activation energy: 95 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Subtract.</p>

  <p>Reduction = 150 kJ − 95 kJ = 55 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The catalyst lowers the activation energy by 55 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Understanding what a catalyst changes</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction has reactants at 200 kJ and products at 130 kJ. A catalyst lowers
    the activation energy from 110 kJ to 65 kJ. Calculate the overall energy change
    and explain whether the catalyst changes it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the overall energy change.</p>

  <p>Energy change = Products − Reactants</p>

  <p>= 130 kJ − 200 kJ = −70 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the sign. The reaction is exothermic.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Consider the catalyst. It lowers the activation-energy
  barrier but does not change the energy levels of the reactants and products.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The overall energy change remains −70 kJ. The
  catalyst changes the reaction pathway, not the overall energy difference.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A catalyst increases reaction rate by providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy.</li>
    <li>A catalyst is not permanently consumed by the overall reaction, although it can become inactive.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the overall energy change between the same reactants and products.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the equilibrium position of a reversible reaction.</li>
    <li>A catalyst usually helps a reaction reach its final state sooner; it does not automatically increase the theoretical amount of product.</li>
  </ul>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define a catalyst and explain how it increases the rate of a chemical reaction.",
      hint: "Include what happens to the reaction pathway and activation energy.",
      steps: [
        "Define a catalyst in terms of its effect on reaction rate.",
        "Explain that it provides an alternative pathway.",
        "State how the activation energy changes.",
        "Connect the lower barrier to successful reactions per second."
      ],
      ans: "A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a reaction without being permanently consumed by the overall reaction. It provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy, allowing a greater proportion of particles to react successfully per second.",
      why: "The catalyst lowers the energy barrier for the reaction rather than changing the overall energy difference between reactants and products."
    },
    {
      q: "An uncatalysed reaction has an activation energy of 175 kJ. With a catalyst, the activation energy is 105 kJ. Calculate the reduction in activation energy.",
      hint: "Subtract the catalysed activation energy from the uncatalysed value.",
      steps: [
        "Write: Reduction = Uncatalysed activation energy − Catalysed activation energy.",
        "Substitute: 175 kJ − 105 kJ.",
        "Calculate the difference."
      ],
      ans: "Reduction = 175 kJ − 105 kJ = 70 kJ.",
      why: "The catalysed pathway requires 70 kJ less activation energy on the stated energy scale."
    },
    {
      q: "Hydrogen peroxide decomposes according to the equation 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂. Explain how manganese(IV) oxide can affect this reaction and state whether it changes the overall chemical equation.",
      hint: "Think about catalytic action and the meaning of an overall balanced equation.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the role of manganese(IV) oxide.",
        "Explain how it affects activation energy and reaction rate.",
        "State whether it changes the reactants and products in the overall equation."
      ],
      ans: "Manganese(IV) oxide catalyses the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide by providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy. It increases the reaction rate but does not change the overall equation: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂.",
      why: "A catalyst changes the pathway and rate of the reaction, not the overall chemical transformation."
    },
    {
      q: "The reactants have an energy of 240 kJ and the products have an energy of 165 kJ. A catalyst lowers the activation energy but does not change either energy level. Calculate the overall energy change and explain why it remains unchanged.",
      hint: "Calculate products' energy minus reactants' energy.",
      steps: [
        "Write: Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants.",
        "Substitute: 165 kJ − 240 kJ.",
        "Calculate and classify the reaction.",
        "Explain why changing the pathway does not change the endpoint energy difference."
      ],
      ans: "Energy change = 165 kJ − 240 kJ = −75 kJ. The reaction is exothermic. The overall energy change remains −75 kJ because the catalyst changes the reaction pathway and activation energy, not the energy levels of the reactants and products.",
      why: "The overall energy change depends on the difference between the reactants and products, whereas activation energy depends on the pathway's energy barrier."
    },
    {
      q: "Describe a fair experiment to determine whether a catalyst increases the rate of decomposition of hydrogen peroxide.",
      hint: "Compare the reaction with and without the catalyst while controlling the other conditions.",
      steps: [
        "Use equal volumes and concentrations of hydrogen peroxide.",
        "Maintain the same starting temperature.",
        "Add a measured amount of catalyst to one experiment and none to the comparison experiment.",
        "Measure oxygen volume at regular time intervals using suitable apparatus.",
        "Compare the rates."
      ],
      ans: "Use equal volumes and concentrations of hydrogen peroxide at the same temperature. Add a measured amount of manganese(IV) oxide to one sample and no catalyst to the other. Measure oxygen production at regular intervals and compare the rates.",
      why: "Keeping the other conditions the same allows the effect of the catalyst to be assessed."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner claims that adding a catalyst to a reversible reaction increases the equilibrium amount of product because the reaction is faster. Explain why this claim is incorrect.",
      hint: "Distinguish the time taken to reach equilibrium from the equilibrium position.",
      steps: [
        "State how a catalyst affects the forward reaction.",
        "State how it affects the reverse reaction.",
        "Explain what happens to the time taken to reach equilibrium.",
        "Distinguish this from the equilibrium composition."
      ],
      ans: "A catalyst speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions by providing lower-activation-energy pathways. It helps the system reach equilibrium sooner, but it does not change the equilibrium position or the equilibrium amounts of reactants and products.",
      why: "Reaction rate describes how quickly change occurs; equilibrium position describes the final balance between forward and reverse reactions."
    }
  ]
);
