add(
  "chemistry",
  "energy_rate_reaction",
  "Heat Energy Calculations",

  `
  <h2>Heat Energy Calculations</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is Heat Energy?</h3>

  <p>
    In these calculations, <strong>heat</strong> refers to thermal energy transferred
    because of a temperature difference. Energy moves from a hotter object or region
    to a colder one unless other processes supply or remove energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    The amount of energy transferred can depend on the mass being heated, the
    substance's specific heat capacity and the temperature change.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Heat Energy Formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q:</strong> thermal energy transferred, in joules (J).</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass, in kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>c:</strong> specific heat capacity, in J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ or J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.</li>
    <li><strong>ΔT:</strong> final temperature minus initial temperature.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This formula applies when the substance changes temperature without changing
    state, and when the specific heat capacity can be treated as constant over
    the temperature interval.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Units and Conversions</h3>

  <p>
    If the specific heat capacity is given per kilogram, mass must be in kilograms.
    Convert grams to kilograms by dividing by 1,000.
  </p>

  <p>For example: 250 g = 250 ÷ 1,000 = 0.250 kg.</p>

  <p>
    Convert joules to kilojoules by dividing by 1,000. Convert kilojoules to joules
    by multiplying by 1,000.
  </p>

  <p>
    The temperature change is the difference between final and initial temperatures.
    A rise from 20°C to 70°C is a change of 50°C, not 70°C.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Heating and Cooling</h3>

  <p>
    When energy is absorbed and no state change occurs, the temperature usually rises.
    When energy is removed, the temperature usually falls.
  </p>

  <p>
    The magnitude of the energy transferred can be calculated using the magnitude
    of the temperature change. If the direction of transfer is required, state
    whether energy was absorbed or released by the object.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practical experiments, not all energy supplied by a heater necessarily enters
    the substance. Some may heat the container or escape to the surroundings.
    A calculation using Q = mcΔT for the substance assumes the stated energy transfer
    is the energy absorbed by that substance.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Converting mass before calculating heat energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calculate the energy required to heat 250 g of water from 20°C to 60°C.
    Use c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Convert mass to kilograms.</p>
  <p>m = 250 ÷ 1,000 = 0.250 kg</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the temperature change.</p>
  <p>ΔT = 60°C − 20°C = 40°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute into Q = mcΔT.</p>
  <p>Q = 0.250 × 4,200 × 40</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 42,000 J = 42 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water absorbs 42,000 J of energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Finding mass from heat energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    A substance with c = 500 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ absorbs 15,000 J as its temperature
    rises by 30°C. Calculate its mass.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write Q = mcΔT.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rearrange to make m the subject.</p>
  <p>m = Q ÷ (cΔT)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>m = 15,000 ÷ (500 × 30)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>m = 15,000 ÷ 15,000 = 1 kg</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mass is 1 kg.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating energy removed during cooling</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 2 kg metal block with c = 450 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ cools from 100°C to 40°C.
    Calculate the magnitude of the thermal energy released.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the temperature decrease.</p>
  <p>Temperature decrease = 100°C − 40°C = 60°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use the magnitude of the temperature change.</p>
  <p>Q = mcΔT = 2 × 450 × 60</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 54,000 J = 54 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The block releases 54,000 J of thermal energy,
  assuming the calculated energy loss is attributed to the block's cooling.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Convert grams to kilograms when using a specific heat capacity per kilogram.</li>
    <li>Use the difference between final and initial temperatures.</li>
    <li>Do not use Q = mcΔT for the energy absorbed during a change of state at constant temperature.</li>
    <li>Distinguish energy absorbed by an object from energy supplied by a heater when heat losses are present.</li>
    <li>Include the correct unit in every final answer.</li>
  </ul>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "State the formula used to calculate heat energy transferred when a substance changes temperature without changing state. Define each symbol.",
      hint: "The formula combines mass, specific heat capacity and temperature change.",
      steps: [
        "Write the formula.",
        "Define Q and its unit.",
        "Define m and its unit.",
        "Define c and its unit.",
        "Define ΔT."
      ],
      ans: "Q = mcΔT, where Q is thermal energy transferred in joules, m is mass in kilograms, c is specific heat capacity in J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ or J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, and ΔT is the final temperature minus the initial temperature.",
      why: "Each quantity determines the energy needed for a particular mass to undergo a specified temperature change."
    },
    {
      q: "Calculate the energy required to heat 400 g of water from 15°C to 45°C. Use c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.",
      hint: "Convert 400 g to kilograms before using Q = mcΔT.",
      steps: [
        "Convert mass: 400 g = 0.400 kg.",
        "Calculate ΔT = 45°C − 15°C = 30°C.",
        "Substitute: Q = 0.400 × 4,200 × 30.",
        "Calculate and state the answer in joules."
      ],
      ans: "Q = 0.400 × 4,200 × 30 = 50,400 J.",
      why: "The formula requires mass in kilograms because the specific heat capacity is given per kilogram."
    },
    {
      q: "A 1.2 kg block with c = 750 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ absorbs 18,000 J. Its initial temperature is 20°C. Calculate its final temperature, assuming no heat loss or change of state.",
      hint: "Calculate ΔT = Q ÷ (mc), then add it to 20°C.",
      steps: [
        "Write ΔT = Q ÷ (mc).",
        "Substitute: ΔT = 18,000 ÷ (1.2 × 750).",
        "Calculate ΔT.",
        "Add the temperature increase to 20°C."
      ],
      ans: "ΔT = 18,000 ÷ 900 = 20°C. Final temperature = 20°C + 20°C = 40°C.",
      why: "The absorbed energy produces a 20°C increase under the stated assumptions."
    },
    {
      q: "A metal block cools from 150°C to 50°C and releases 90,000 J. Its mass is 2 kg. Calculate its specific heat capacity.",
      hint: "Find the temperature decrease and rearrange Q = mcΔT to make c the subject.",
      steps: [
        "Calculate the magnitude of the temperature change: 150°C − 50°C = 100°C.",
        "Use c = Q ÷ (mΔT).",
        "Substitute: c = 90,000 ÷ (2 × 100).",
        "Calculate the specific heat capacity."
      ],
      ans: "c = 90,000 ÷ 200 = 450 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.",
      why: "The energy released, mass and temperature decrease determine the specific heat capacity."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why a calculation using Q = mcΔT may give a different value from the electrical energy supplied by a heater.",
      hint: "Consider where the supplied energy goes besides the substance being heated.",
      steps: [
        "Identify what Q = mcΔT calculates for the substance.",
        "Consider energy absorbed by the container.",
        "Consider energy transferred to the surroundings.",
        "Explain the difference between energy supplied and energy absorbed by the substance."
      ],
      ans: "Q = mcΔT calculates the thermal energy associated with the substance's temperature change. Some electrical energy may heat the container or be lost to the surroundings, so the total energy supplied by the heater can be greater than the energy absorbed by the substance.",
      why: "Energy supplied to the entire apparatus is not necessarily equal to energy transferred into the measured substance."
    },
    {
      q: "A 0.75 kg substance with c = 400 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ cools from 90°C to 30°C. Calculate the thermal energy released in joules and kilojoules.",
      hint: "Use the magnitude of the temperature decrease in Q = mcΔT.",
      steps: [
        "Calculate the temperature decrease: 90°C − 30°C = 60°C.",
        "Substitute: Q = 0.75 × 400 × 60.",
        "Calculate the energy in joules.",
        "Divide by 1,000 to convert joules to kilojoules."
      ],
      ans: "Q = 0.75 × 400 × 60 = 18,000 J. In kilojoules, Q = 18 kJ.",
      why: "The magnitude gives the energy released as the substance cools through 60°C."
    }
  ]
);


