// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 16. NON-ELECTROLYTES
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "electrochemistry",
  "Non-electrolytes",

  `
  <h2>Non-electrolytes</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Non-electrolyte</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>non-electrolyte</strong> is a substance that does not
    produce a significant concentration of mobile ions when dissolved
    in water. Its solution therefore does not conduct electricity
    effectively through ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many non-electrolytes are molecular substances. When they dissolve,
    their molecules remain largely as neutral particles instead of
    separating into positive and negative ions.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Example: Sugar in Water</h3>

  <p>
    Ordinary table sugar, sucrose, dissolves in water. Its molecules
    spread throughout the water, but they do not normally split into
    ions simply because the sugar dissolves.
  </p>

  <p><strong>C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(s) → C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>
    This equation represents sucrose molecules dissolving. It does not
    show the formation of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A sugar solution therefore contains mostly neutral sugar molecules
    and water molecules. It has very few ions from the sugar itself and
    conducts electricity very poorly compared with an electrolyte solution.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Example: Ethanol in Water</h3>

  <p>
    Ethanol mixes with water, but its molecules remain largely neutral.
    Dissolving ethanol does not normally produce a substantial
    concentration of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consequently, ethanol solution is a poor conductor of electricity
    compared with solutions containing mobile ions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Why Dissolving Does Not Always Produce Ions</h3>

  <p>
    Dissolving means that a substance mixes with a solvent to form a
    solution. Ion formation is a separate process.
  </p>

  <p>
    An ionic substance may dissociate into ions when it dissolves.
    Some molecular substances ionise in water. Other molecular
    substances, such as sucrose, dissolve without forming significant
    numbers of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, a substance can dissolve readily in water and still be
    a non-electrolyte.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Comparing the Three Types</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Strong electrolyte:</strong> Forms ions extensively.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weak electrolyte:</strong> Forms ions partially.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Non-electrolyte:</strong> Does not form a significant
      concentration of ions under the conditions considered.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These categories describe ion formation, not simply whether a
    substance dissolves or whether it is present in a large amount.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining sugar solution</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sugar dissolves in water, but the resulting solution conducts
    electricity very poorly. Explain why.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particles in the solution.</p>
  <p>Sugar molecules and water molecules are present.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Determine whether sugar forms ions.</p>
  <p>Sucrose dissolves largely as neutral molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Relate the particles to conduction.</p>
  <p>There are very few mobile ions supplied by the sugar.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sugar is a non-electrolyte because it dissolves
  without producing a significant concentration of mobile ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Distinguishing dissolving from ionisation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why the statement “every substance that dissolves in water
    conducts electricity” is incorrect.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Define dissolving.</p>
  <p>Dissolving distributes particles of a substance throughout a solvent.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the type of particles formed.</p>
  <p>Some substances form mobile ions; others remain as neutral molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Link mobile ions to conductivity.</p>
  <p>A solution needs mobile charge carriers for effective ionic conduction.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A substance may dissolve as neutral molecules
  without forming enough ions to conduct electricity effectively.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Classifying ethanol solution</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ethanol mixes with water but remains largely in molecular form.
    Classify the solution in terms of electrolyte behaviour.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particles formed.</p>
  <p>Ethanol molecules remain largely neutral in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Check whether significant ion formation occurs.</p>
  <p>Ethanol does not produce a significant concentration of ions under
  ordinary aqueous conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethanol is treated as a non-electrolyte.</p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define a non-electrolyte and explain why its aqueous solution conducts electricity very poorly.",
      hint: "Focus on the number of mobile ions produced.",
      steps: [
        "Define a non-electrolyte in terms of ion formation.",
        "Explain what happens to its particles when it dissolves.",
        "Relate the scarcity of mobile ions to electrical conduction."
      ],
      ans: "A non-electrolyte does not produce a significant concentration of mobile ions in water. Its particles remain largely neutral molecules, so the solution conducts electricity very poorly through ions.",
      why: "Effective ionic conduction requires mobile charged particles."
    },
    {
      q: "Sugar dissolves in water, but sugar solution conducts electricity very poorly. Explain this observation.",
      hint: "Consider whether dissolved sucrose molecules become ions.",
      steps: [
        "State that sucrose dissolves in water.",
        "Identify the particles present after dissolving.",
        "Explain why these particles do not carry ionic current effectively."
      ],
      ans: "Sucrose dissolves largely as neutral molecules rather than separating into ions. Because the solution contains very few ions from the sugar, it conducts electricity very poorly.",
      why: "Dissolving does not necessarily produce ions."
    },
    {
      q: "Write a simple equation representing sucrose dissolving in water, and explain why it is not an ionisation equation.",
      hint: "The formula of sucrose is C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁.",
      steps: [
        "Write solid sucrose on the left.",
        "Write aqueous sucrose on the right.",
        "Check whether the equation shows charged products."
      ],
      ans: "C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(s) → C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(aq). This represents dissolution because the sucrose remains molecular and no ions are shown as products.",
      why: "The aqueous symbol indicates that sucrose is dissolved; it does not mean that it has ionised."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain the difference between dissolving and ionisation using a substance that behaves as a non-electrolyte.",
      hint: "Compare the distribution of particles in water with the formation of charged particles.",
      steps: [
        "Explain what happens during dissolving.",
        "Explain what happens during ionisation.",
        "Use sucrose as an example to distinguish the processes."
      ],
      ans: "Dissolving distributes a substance through a solvent. Ionisation forms ions from neutral particles. Sucrose dissolves in water but remains largely as neutral molecules, so dissolving does not cause significant ion formation.",
      why: "A solution can form without producing the mobile ions needed for effective ionic conduction."
    },
    {
      q: "Ethanol mixes with water but does not produce a significant concentration of ions. Classify ethanol and explain your answer.",
      hint: "Classify it according to the particles present in the solution.",
      steps: [
        "Identify ethanol as a molecular substance.",
        "State that its molecules remain largely neutral in water.",
        "Relate this to the definition of a non-electrolyte."
      ],
      ans: "Ethanol is a non-electrolyte under ordinary aqueous conditions because it dissolves without producing a significant concentration of mobile ions.",
      why: "Electrolyte classification depends on ion formation, not simply on whether a substance mixes with water."
    },
    {
      q: "Compare a strong electrolyte, a weak electrolyte and a non-electrolyte in terms of ion formation and electrical conduction.",
      hint: "Compare extensive, partial and insignificant ion formation.",
      steps: [
        "Describe ion formation in a strong electrolyte.",
        "Describe ion formation in a weak electrolyte.",
        "Describe ion formation in a non-electrolyte.",
        "Relate the mobile ions present to their typical electrical conduction."
      ],
      ans: "A strong electrolyte forms ions extensively and generally conducts well. A weak electrolyte forms ions partially and generally conducts less effectively at comparable concentration. A non-electrolyte forms no significant concentration of ions and conducts very poorly through ions.",
      why: "The extent to which mobile ions are produced is central to comparing these three types of substances."
    }
  ]
);

